  "response_text": "Say that the number of threads we know we want to create is equal to N.\\nBefore any memory is allocated to these stacks, we should ensure that the requested memory does not exceed the MAX_STACK_SIZE determined by the programmer or the system.\\nWe should then declare compile-time constants that determine the size of each type of stack (i.e. MAIN_STACK_SIZE, PROCESS_STACK_SIZE).\\nIt would also be good to keep track of the sizes of all of the stacks in a list of size N (e.g. thread_stack_sizes[]). This is so that we don\u2019t index to the bottom of a non-empty stack when switching between threads.\\nAssuming we have found the initial MSP via dereferencing the double pointer at 0x00 (uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;) we can find calculate the pointer for the top of the i-th thread\u2019s stack with the equation:\\nith_thread_ptr = MSP_INIT_VAL \u2013 MAIN_STACK_SIZE \u2013 (i*PROCESS_STACK_SIZE) \u2013 thread_size[i]\\nHere we are indexing the threads starting from zero. Also note that we are subtracting the value from the main stack\u2019s address, so that it can more effectively share the memory space with the heap, which grows upwards.\\n",
